# PUT_PC carrying an LTR message.
#
# HAND BUILT FROM THE SPECIFICATION. See put_pc_memory_write32.espi.
#
# The Message cycle type is the one peripheral packet whose Length field is
# neither a byte count nor a 4 KB flag. Page 55: "For Message cycle type, the
# Length field is Reserved and it must be sent with all 0s." That sentence is
# nowhere near Table 5 and nowhere near Figure 38 -- it is in the prose between
# the completion figure and Table 6.
#
# Table 6, p.55, is a one-row table the plan never mentions: LTR is the only
# message code the base specification defines, and it decides what the four
# message specific bytes of Figure 38 mean.
#
#   00     PUT_PC
#   10     Cycle Type: 0001 r2r1r0 0 with r2r1r0 = 000b
#          Message, routing "Local -- terminated at receiver" (note 5, p.49)
#   00     Tag 0, Length[11:8] = 0h
#   00     Length[7:0] = 00h                Figure 40 draws these two as the
#                                           literal cells 0h and 00h rather
#                                           than as fields, because LTR "uses
#                                           Message cycle Type with no data
#                                           payload" (p.56).
#   01     Message Code = LTR               Table 6, p.55
#   89     RQ=1, RSV=00b, LS=010b, LV[9:8]=01b     Figure 40, p.56
#   2C     LV[7:0] = 2Ch
#   00 00  Reserved                         Figure 40 bytes 6 and 7
#
# LS=010b is "Value times 1,024 ns" and LV is 01_0010_1100b = 300, so the
# reported tolerance is 307,200 ns. Table 7, p.57.
#
# CRC bytes pasted from tools/crc_ref.py (rule R4).
CMD 00 10 00 00 01 89 2C 00 00 7B
TAR
RSP 08 0F 01 95

# The same message with the Requirement bit clear. Table 7, p.57: "A '0'
# indicates that eSPI target has no service requirement. When this bit is a
# '1', the remaining fields are valid to indicate latency tolerance requirement
# for the eSPI target."
#
# So the scale and value below are not a zero-nanosecond tolerance -- they are
# fields the target said not to read, and printing "0 ns" here would invent a
# requirement out of a target explicitly disclaiming one. Same shape of mistake
# as reading a virtual wire level under a clear valid bit, which the capture
# caught in stage C.
CMD 00 10 00 00 01 00 00 00 00 55
TAR
RSP 08 0F 01 95
